---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const title = 'About';
const description =
  'Snippet Tester is one person doing SEO and answer-engine work, arguing from published evidence rather than urgency. Here is the reasoning, and the conflict of interest.';
---
<Base title={title} description={description}>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
      <h1>One person, an argument, and a stated conflict of interest.</h1>

      <p class="lede">
        Snippet Tester is a one-person practice. You are talking to the person who does the work,
        which is the only real advantage a solo operator has and I would rather lead with it than
        pretend to be a team.
      </p>

      <h2>Why the evidence-first angle</h2>
      <p>
        This domain used to host a product. It was a tool for A/B testing search snippets, and it got
        to a working beta before I killed it — because Google Search Console already reported the same
        numbers and my tool added a scheduler and a login screen. It solved nothing that was not
        already solved for free.
      </p>
      <p>
        Making that call taught me the thing this practice is built on. The search industry is full of
        products and services that are technically real and practically pointless, and the only way to
        tell them apart is to look for evidence and be willing to accept the answer. I applied that to
        my own product and it failed. So the evidence base on this site is not a marketing device
        bolted on afterwards — it is the same test, run on everything else in the category.
        <a href="/teardowns/auditing-our-own-abandoned-product">The first teardown I published is of the old site</a>,
        for the same reason.
      </p>

      <h2>What I actually believe about AI search</h2>
      <p>
        Compressed to four sentences, so you can disagree with it before contacting me.
      </p>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">Position</th><th scope="col">Confidence</th><th scope="col">Basis</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>AI chatbots are not meaningfully taking your traffic yet — all of them combined send about 0.29% of search referrals, against Google's 87.6%.</td>
              <td><span class="badge badge--ok">High</span></td>
              <td class="small"><a href="https://technologychecker.io/blog/search-engine-market-share" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">Referral share data, July 2026</a></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Google's own AI Overviews are taking your clicks, measurably, and that is the actual problem.</td>
              <td><span class="badge badge--ok">High</span></td>
              <td class="small"><a href="https://ahrefs.com/blog/ai-overviews-reduce-clicks/" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">Ahrefs</a>, <a href="https://searchengineland.com/google-ai-overviews-ctr-recovery-study-475566" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">Seer via Search Engine Land</a> — five studies, -15.5% to -61%</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>The fixes with evidence behind them are structural and editorial: answer first, facts in tables, figures cited, headings ordered.</td>
              <td><span class="badge badge--mid">Moderate</span></td>
              <td class="small"><a href="https://www.frase.io/blog/what-is-answer-engine-optimization-the-complete-guide-to-getting-cited-by-ai" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">Correlational: +22% statistics, +37% quotations, 2.8x heading order</a></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Much of what is sold as AEO — llms.txt files being the clearest case — has no measurable effect.</td>
              <td><span class="badge badge--ok">High</span></td>
              <td class="small"><a href="https://ppc.land/llms-txt-adoption-rises-8-8x-but-97-of-files-get-zero-ai-requests/" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">97% zero requests across 137,000 domains</a></td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        All four are argued with sources on <a href="/evidence">the evidence page</a>. If new research
        contradicts one, it changes there and the date at the top moves.
      </p>

      <h2>The conflict of interest</h2>
      <p>
        I sell the services described on this site, which means I benefit from you believing AI search
        matters. You should read everything here with that in mind. The specific way I try not to abuse
        it: the free checker will tell you when your page is fine, the services page lists who should
        not hire me, and the evidence page publishes findings against my own category. None of that
        eliminates the bias. It just makes it checkable.
      </p>

      <h2>How this site is built</h2>
      <p>
        Static HTML generated by Astro, hosted on a CDN, no web fonts, no analytics scripts, no cookie
        banner, no consent flow, nothing to consent to. The free checker runs as a single serverless
        function that fetches your page and its robots.txt and scores eight signals with regular
        expressions — no model, no API call, no per-run cost. That is why it is free and why it always
        returns the same score for the same page.
      </p>
      <p>
        It is also the argument. If someone wants to sell you page performance, the first evidence
        should be their own.
      </p>

      <div class="btn-row" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
        <a class="btn btn--accent" href="/#checker">Run the checker</a>
        <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="mailto:hello@snippettester.com">hello@snippettester.com</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
